/* Índice del buscador en inglés (generado por _src-publicaciones/ingles/indice_en.py, no editar a mano). */
window.CATTO_INDICE = (window.CATTO_INDICE || []).concat([{"t":"The best-measured quantity","d":"Time and frequency measurements","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/tiempo-frecuencia#patrones","x":"01 The best-measured quantity Time is the quantity that is measured with the greatest accuracy. Since 1967, the second of the International System has been defined by the radiation of cesium 133: 9,192,631,770 periods of a transition of its atom. The cesium atomic clocks of the national laboratories realize it with uncertainties of 10⁻¹⁵ or less, and that reference reaches measurement laboratories through radio signals, networks and satellites. Reference Typical accuracy Use Ordinary crystal (XO) 10 to 50 ppm Microcontrollers, clocks Temperature-compensated crystal (TCXO) 0.5 to 2 ppm Phones, GPS receivers Oven-controlled crystal (OCXO) 0.001 to 0.1 ppm Laboratory instruments Rubidium 10⁻¹⁰ to 10⁻¹¹ Base stations, laboratories GPS-disciplined oscillator 10⁻¹² over long averages Traceable laboratory reference Besides accuracy, stability matters: how much the frequency changes over a given interval. It is expressed with the Allan deviation for each averaging time; a crystal is very stable over one second but drifts over hours, and GPS the other way around: that is why they are combined in disciplined oscillators."},{"t":"The direct-counting counter","d":"Time and frequency measurements","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/tiempo-frecuencia#directo","x":"02 The direct-counting counter The simplest counter opens a gate for a time \\( T_g \\), derived from its timebase, and counts the edges of the signal. Since the gate is not synchronized with the signal, one edge more or one less can get in: the ±1 count error. The relative resolution is: \\[ \\frac{\\delta f}{f} = \\frac{1}{f\\, T_g} \\] With a 1 s gate, 1 Hz is resolved: excellent for 100 MHz (10⁻⁸), useless for 10 Hz (10%). For low frequencies it is better to measure the period."},{"t":"The reciprocal counter","d":"Time and frequency measurements","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/tiempo-frecuencia#reciproco","x":"03 The reciprocal counter The reciprocal counter synchronizes the gate with the edges of the signal: it counts an integer number of periods of the input and, at the same time, measures with the timebase, and a high-resolution interpolator, the exact time they lasted. The frequency is the quotient. Its resolution no longer depends on the input frequency, but on the time resolution \\( t_{res} \\) of the interpolator: \\[ \\frac{\\delta f}{f} \\approx \\frac{t_{res}}{T_g} \\] With 100 ps of resolution and a 1 s gate, 10⁻¹⁰: about ten digits per second, at any frequency. The same instruments measure time intervals between two signals with the same resolution. No method outdoes its timebase: the relative error of the reference adds directly to the result. That is why laboratory counters have an external 10 MHz reference input, to share a better standard. Lab · direct and reciprocal counting The relative resolution of the two methods as a function of frequency, for the chosen gate time (the reciprocal one with a 100 ps interpolator). The horizontal line is the timebase error; the uncertainty of each method is the sum. Measured frequency = 10 ms gate 100 ms gate 1 s gate 10 s gate Timebase: crystal (10 ppm) TCXO (1 ppm) OCXO (0.01 ppm) GPS-disciplined (10⁻¹¹)"},{"t":"In the laboratory","d":"Time and frequency measurements","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/tiempo-frecuencia#laboratorio","x":"04 In the laboratory Activity 1 · ±1 count Measure a 50 Hz signal with a direct-counting counter and a reciprocal one, with several gates, and compare the scatter of the readings. Activity 2 · External reference Measure the frequency of a generator with the counter free-running and with both instruments locked to the same 10 MHz reference. Activity 3 · Drift of a crystal Record for an hour the frequency of an ordinary crystal oscillator while warming it with your hand, against a GPS-disciplined reference."},{"t":"Common mistakes","d":"Time and frequency measurements","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/tiempo-frecuencia#errores","x":"05 Common mistakes Measuring low frequencies by direct counting. The ±1 count error dominates; it is better to measure the period or use a reciprocal counter. Trusting every digit on the display. The timebase limits the accuracy even if the resolution is high. Confusing accuracy with stability. An oscillator can be very stable and be off frequency. Forgetting the noise at the trigger. Signal noise shifts the edges and adds uncertainty."},{"t":"Self-assessment","d":"Time and frequency measurements","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/tiempo-frecuencia#autoevaluacion","x":"06 Self-assessment What resolution does a direct counter give with a 0.1 s gate? 10 Hz. A counter with a 1 ppm TCXO measures 100 MHz. What is the timebase error? 100 Hz. Why does the reciprocal counter have the same resolution at any frequency? Because it measures the time of an integer number of periods, and its resolution depends on the interpolator, not on the frequency. How many cesium periods define the second? 9,192,631,770."},{"t":"Further reading","d":"Time and frequency measurements","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/tiempo-frecuencia#ampliar","x":"07 Further reading Michael A. Lombardi. Fundamentals of Time and Frequency. In The Mechatronics Handbook, CRC Press, 2002; also published by NIST. A clear introduction to standards, stability and traceability. Keysight Technologies. Fundamentals of the Electronic Counters. Application Note 200, Keysight. Direct counting, reciprocal counting and time interval measurement. David W. Allan. Statistics of Atomic Frequency Standards. Proceedings of the IEEE, 54(2), 1966. The paper that introduced the Allan variance."},{"t":"What a safe work procedure is","d":"Safe work procedures","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/trabajo-seguro#procedimiento","x":"01 What a safe work procedure is A safe work procedure describes step by step how to carry out a risky task: what has to be prepared, what hazards appear at each step, what controls are applied and who gives authorization. It is built with a job safety analysis (JSA): the task is divided into steps, the hazards of each one are listed and a measure is assigned following the hierarchy of controls from industrial hygiene and safety. For the highest-risk tasks a work permit is also used: a document signed by the person who authorizes the work, who verifies the conditions before starting, and which is valid for a defined time and place. Task Main hazard Typical permit controls Hot work Fire from sparks or flame Remove combustibles, fire extinguisher at hand, fire watch afterwards Work at height Fall Harness with an anchor point, inspected scaffolding, barrier below Confined space Toxic or oxygen-deficient atmosphere Gas testing, ventilation, attendant outside, rescue plan Electrical work Electric shock and arc Lockout with the five golden rules, arc-rated PPE"},{"t":"Lockout: the five golden rules","d":"Safe work procedures","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/trabajo-seguro#consignacion","x":"02 Lockout: the five golden rules To work de-energized on an electrical installation it is not enough to open a switch. Lockout follows five steps in order, and the work starts only when all of them have been completed: Disconnect all sources of voltage, with a visible or reliable break. Lock the switching devices with a padlock and tag them with a card, so that no one closes them. Each person who works puts on their own padlock. Verify the absence of voltage with a detector tested before and after on a known source. Ground and short-circuit all the conductors, to discharge capacitances and to protect against an accidental energization. Mark off and signpost the work area. The same principle of lockout and tagout applies to every hazardous energy: pneumatic, hydraulic, mechanical or thermal. In electronics it also appears in the filter capacitors of power supplies and drives, which keep lethal voltages for minutes after disconnection: they must be discharged and verified."},{"t":"Electric shock and protection by a residual-current device","d":"Safe work procedures","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/trabajo-seguro#electrico","x":"03 Electric shock and protection by a residual-current device The harm is produced by the current that passes through the body, and it depends on its magnitude, duration and path. Standard IEC 60479-1 divides the time–current plane into zones: below half a milliampere it is not perceived; around 10 mA there are contractions that prevent letting go of the conductor, and with a few tens of milliamperes for fractions of a second there is a risk of ventricular fibrillation, which is the main cause of death by electrocution. In an installation with a TT system, the neutral is grounded at the transformer and the exposed conductive parts of the installation are connected to their own ground rod. If a phase touches the enclosure of a device, the fault current returns through the earth, and the enclosure is left at a voltage with respect to the ground: \\[ I_f = \\frac{U_0}{R_A + R_B} \\] \\[ U_c = I_f\\, R_A \\] \\( U_0 \\) = 220 V is the phase voltage, \\( R_A \\) the resistance of the grounding of the exposed conductive parts and \\( R_B \\) that of the neutral. With resistances of a few ohms, the fault current is a few amperes: it does not make a 16 A circuit breaker trip, and the enclosure stays at more than 100 V for as long as the fault lasts. That is why in the TT system the protection is provided by the residual-current device (RCD), which detects the difference between the current leaving through the phase and the current returning through the neutral. With a 30 mA device, the condition"},{"t":"In the laboratory","d":"Safe work procedures","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/trabajo-seguro#laboratorio","x":"04 In the laboratory Activity 1 · JSA Write the job safety analysis for replacing a filter capacitor in the 400 V supply of a variable-speed drive. Activity 2 · Testing an RCD With an RCD tester, measure the trip current and trip time of the laboratory’s circuit breakers and compare them with the values in the standard. Activity 3 · Measuring the grounding Measure the resistance of the laboratory’s ground rod with an earth resistance tester using the fall-of-potential method and verify the condition \\( R_A \\cdot 30\\ \\mathrm{mA} \\le 50 \\) V."},{"t":"Common mistakes","d":"Safe work procedures","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/trabajo-seguro#errores","x":"05 Common mistakes Trusting that an open switch means no voltage. It has to be verified, and there may be backfeed from another source. Using a single padlock for several people. Each person locks out with their own and removes it themselves. Believing that the circuit breaker protects people. It protects the cables; in a TT system a ground fault does not make it trip. Forgetting charged capacitors. A disconnected power supply can have hundreds of volts stored."},{"t":"Self-assessment","d":"Safe work procedures","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/trabajo-seguro#autoevaluacion","x":"06 Self-assessment What is the maximum grounding resistance of the exposed parts with a 30 mA RCD and a 50 V limit voltage? \\( 50/0.03 \\approx 1667 \\) Ω. With R A = 20 Ω and R B = 5 Ω, what fault current flows and at what voltage is the enclosure left? 8.8 A and 176 V. Why are the conductors grounded and short-circuited after verifying the absence of voltage? To discharge capacitances, and so that an accidental energization trips the protections with no voltage in the work area. Which tasks require a work permit? The high-risk ones: hot work, work at height, in confined spaces and electrical work, among others."},{"t":"Further reading","d":"Safe work procedures","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/trabajo-seguro#ampliar","x":"07 Further reading IEC. IEC 60479-1: Effects of current on human beings and livestock. International Electrotechnical Commission. The time–current zones and the impedance of the human body. Asociación Electrotécnica Argentina (Argentine Electrotechnical Association). Reglamentación para la ejecución de instalaciones eléctricas en inmuebles (AEA 90364) (Regulations for the execution of electrical installations in buildings) (in Spanish). AEA, Buenos Aires. The earthing system types and protection against indirect contact. NFPA. NFPA 70E: Standard for Electrical Safety in the Workplace. National Fire Protection Association. Safe electrical work practices, lockout/tagout and arc flash protection."},{"t":"Functions that respect the structure","d":"Linear transformations","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/transformaciones-lineales#que-es","x":"01 Functions that respect the structure A linear transformation is a function between vector spaces that plays well with the two operations: \\[ T (\\vec{u} + \\vec{v}) = T (\\vec{u}) + T (\\vec{v}) \\quad T (k \\vec{u}) = k \\, T (\\vec{u}) \\] The two conditions together boil down to one: T(a·u + b·v) = a·T(u) + b·T(v). It is the superposition principle. From the second condition, with k = 0, it follows that T(0) = 0: every linear transformation sends the zero vector to the zero vector. That rules out f(x) = x + 1 right away, which translates, and f(x) = x², which does not respect scaling. Rotations, scalings, reflections, projections, the derivative and the integral are linear. Why it matters to an electronics engineer A circuit made of resistors, capacitors, inductors and linear controlled sources is a linear transformation from input to output. That is why superposition holds: you turn off everything except one source, solve, and then add the results. And that is why a linear circuit driven with a sinusoid responds with a sinusoid of the same frequency: the amplitude and phase change, not the shape."},{"t":"Every linear transformation fits in a matrix","d":"Linear transformations","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/transformaciones-lineales#matriz","x":"02 Every linear transformation fits in a matrix If you know what T does to the vectors of a basis, you already know what it does to any vector: you write it as a linear combination of the basis and apply linearity. Writing the images of the basis as columns gives the associated matrix: \\[ A = \\left[ \\begin{array}{cc} | & | \\\\ T (\\hat{i}) & T (\\hat{j}) \\\\ | & | \\end{array} \\right] \\quad T (X) = A \\cdot X \\] Applying the transformation is multiplying by the matrix. Two different objects —a function and a table of numbers— that turn out to be the same thing. The matrix depends on the chosen bases. The same transformation, seen in another basis, has another matrix: they are similar matrices, related by A′ = P −1 AP. Choosing the right basis so that the matrix ends up as simple as possible is the goal of diagonalization."},{"t":"The transformations of the plane, one by one","d":"Linear transformations","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/transformaciones-lineales#galeria","x":"03 The transformations of the plane, one by one Transformation Matrix det What it does to the plane Rotation by angle φ [[cos φ, −sin φ], [sin φ, cos φ]] 1 Turns everything without deforming it; preserves areas and angles Scaling [[k₁, 0], [0, k₂]] k₁k₂ Stretches each axis separately Reflection across the x axis [[1, 0], [0, −1]] −1 Mirrors; the negative sign indicates that it reverses orientation Shear [[1, k], [0, 1]] 1 Slides the horizontal layers; preserves area but deforms angles Projection onto the x axis [[1, 0], [0, 0]] 0 Flattens the plane onto a line: it loses a dimension and has no inverse Lab · transforming the plane The light figure is the original and the colored one is its image. The transformed grid shows what happens to the whole plane. A =; Rotation 45° Scaling Reflection Shear Projection Identity"},{"t":"Kernel, image and the rank-nullity theorem","d":"Linear transformations","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/transformaciones-lineales#nucleo-imagen","x":"04 Kernel, image and the rank-nullity theorem Kernel The vectors that the transformation sends to zero: Ker(T) = {X: A·X = 0}. It is the solution space of the homogeneous system. Its dimension is called the nullity. Image Everything the transformation can produce: Im(T) = {A·X}. It is spanned by the columns of A, and its dimension is the rank. \\[ \\dim (\\operatorname{Ker}) + \\dim (\\operatorname{Im}) = \\dim (V) \\] Rank-nullity theorem: what is lost plus what survives equals what there was. It is the same count that appeared with the rank of a matrix. The projection of the plane onto the x axis has a kernel of dimension 1 —the whole y axis is flattened to the origin— and an image of dimension 1: 1 + 1 = 2 ✔. A transformation is injective exactly when its kernel is only the zero vector, and in that case, if it also goes from one space to another of the same dimension, it is an isomorphism: it has an inverse, and the matrix of the inverse is A −1."},{"t":"Composing is multiplying","d":"Linear transformations","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/transformaciones-lineales#composicion","x":"05 Composing is multiplying Applying T first and then S is equivalent to multiplying the matrices, in the order in which they are applied, from right to left: the matrix of S ∘ T is A S · A T. That is the origin of the fact that matrix multiplication is not commutative: rotating and then reflecting is not the same as reflecting and then rotating. It is exactly what happens with two stages of a circuit in cascade, which was seen in matrices: each stage is a linear transformation and connecting them is composing them."},{"t":"Where they show up in electronics","d":"Linear transformations","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/transformaciones-lineales#electronica","x":"06 Where they show up in electronics Superposition. It is linearity, nothing more. It holds only in linear circuits: a diode or a transistor in large-signal operation breaks the property, and that is why you have to linearize around an operating point before using it. Filters. A filter is a linear operator on signals. Its “matrix”, in the frequency domain, is the transfer function, which multiplies each spectral component. Discrete Fourier transform. The N-point DFT is, literally, multiplication by an N × N matrix whose elements are roots of unity. The FFT is the algorithm that does that product in N log N operations instead of N². Graphics and CAD. Rotating, scaling and projecting a model on screen are chained linear transformations: a single matrix summarizes the whole chain."},{"t":"In the lab","d":"Linear transformations","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/transformaciones-lineales#laboratorio","x":"07 In the lab Exercise 1 · Building the matrix Find the matrix of the transformation of R² that rotates by 90° and then doubles the x component. Apply it to three vectors and verify with the lab above. Check that composing in the reverse order gives a different matrix. Exercise 2 · Kernel and image For A = [[1, 2], [2, 4]], find the kernel by solving A·X = 0, describe the image and verify the rank-nullity theorem. Explain what happens to the grid in the lab with that matrix. Exercise 3 · Measured superposition In a circuit with two sources, measure the output voltage with each source separately (the other one turned off: the voltage source short-circuited, the current source open-circuited) and then with both together. Check that the sum matches. Repeat adding a diode and explain why it no longer holds."},{"t":"Common mistakes","d":"Linear transformations","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/transformaciones-lineales#errores","x":"08 Common mistakes Believing that every function “with a simple formula” is linear. f(x) = x + 1 is not: it does not send zero to zero. Building the matrix with the images of the basis as rows instead of columns. Reversing the order when composing: the matrix of S ∘ T is A S ·A T. Confusing kernel with image, or their dimensions. Applying superposition to a circuit with nonlinear elements. Forgetting that the matrix depends on the basis: two different matrices can be the same transformation."},{"t":"Self-assessment","d":"Linear transformations","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/transformaciones-lineales#autoevaluacion","x":"09 Self-assessment Is T(x, y) = (2x − y, 0) linear? Yes. Its matrix is [[2, −1], [0, 0]]: it satisfies both conditions and sends zero to zero. What is the matrix of the 90° counterclockwise rotation? [[0, −1], [1, 0]]: it sends î to (0,1) and ĵ to (−1,0). If A is 3 × 3 with rank 2, what is the dimension of the kernel? 3 − 2 = 1: a line of vectors ends up at the origin. When does a transformation from R n to R n have an inverse? When its kernel is only the zero vector, which is equivalent to det(A) ≠ 0 and to rank n. What does it mean for the determinant of the matrix to be −1? That it preserves areas but reverses orientation: there is a reflection. Why does superposition not hold with a transistor in switching operation? Because its behavior is not linear: doubling the input does not double the output, and the response to the sum of two inputs is not the sum of the responses."},{"t":"Further reading","d":"Linear transformations","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/transformaciones-lineales#ampliar","x":"10 Further reading David C. Lay. Linear Algebra and Its Applications. 5th ed. (Spanish edition, “Álgebra lineal y sus aplicaciones”), Pearson, 2016. Develops linear transformations from the matrix, with many figures of the transformed plane. Stanley I. Grossman. Álgebra lineal. 6th ed., McGraw-Hill, 2008 (in Spanish). Kernel, image, the rank-nullity theorem and similar matrices, with complete proofs. Gilbert Strang. Linear Algebra and Its Applications. 4th ed. (Spanish edition, “Álgebra lineal y sus aplicaciones”), Cengage Learning, 2007. The “the matrix is the transformation” point of view, and the chapter on the Fourier transform as a change of basis."},{"t":"Three operations and nothing more","d":"Transformations of the independent variable","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/transformaciones-variable#tres","x":"01 Three operations and nothing more Everything that can be done to the time axis of a signal is built from three moves: Operation Expression What it does Shifting \\( x(t - t_0) \\) With \\( t_0 \\gt 0 \\) it moves to the right: it is a delay Reflection \\( x(-t) \\) Mirrors about the vertical axis: the future becomes the past Scaling \\( x(at) \\) With \\( |a| \\gt 1 \\) it compresses; with \\( |a| \\lt 1 \\), it expands The first two rows come with their own sign trap. That \\( x(t - t_0) \\) moves to the right seems backwards, but it makes sense if you think about where what used to happen at zero happens now: it takes \\( t = t_0 \\) for the argument to equal zero. And the same thing happens with scaling: multiplying the argument by 2 shortens the signal to half, because the argument reaches each value sooner."},{"t":"The combined case, where everybody goes wrong","d":"Transformations of the independent variable","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/transformaciones-variable#combinada","x":"02 The combined case, where everybody goes wrong When they appear together, \\( y(t) = x(at + b) \\), the order matters and the shift is not \\( b \\). Factoring out \\( a \\) makes it plain: \\[ x(at + b) = x\\!\\left(a\\left(t + \\frac{b}{a}\\right)\\right) \\] First shift by \\( b/a \\) —not \\( b \\)— and then scale by \\( a \\). Confusing the two is the most repeated mistake in the subject. There is a method that never fails and spares you from having to remember the order: track the key points. If the original signal has a corner, a jump or a peak at \\( t = t_1 \\), that same feature appears in the transformed signal where the argument equals \\( t_1 \\): \\[ a\\,t + b = t_1 \\;\\Longrightarrow\\; t = \\frac{t_1 - b}{a} \\] With two or three key points mapped, the whole signal is already determined, with no arguments about order or signs. That is what the lab does: it shows where each corner ends up. Lab · where each point ends up Above, the original signal with its three key points; below, \\( x(at+b) \\) with the same points mapped. Move \\( a \\) and \\( b \\) and watch what happens to each one. a = 1.0 b = 0.0 Show the intermediate step"},{"t":"In discrete time, scaling is something else","d":"Transformations of the independent variable","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/transformaciones-variable#discreto","x":"03 In discrete time, scaling is something else Shifting and reflection work the same way: \\( x[n - n_0] \\) and \\( x[-n] \\) hold no surprises, except that \\( n_0 \\) must be an integer. Scaling, on the other hand, changes in nature. Operation Name What happens \\( x[Mn] \\), \\( M \\) an integer Decimation Samples are discarded. Information is lost: it cannot be undone \\( x[n/L] \\), \\( L \\) an integer Interpolation Gaps appear that have to be filled in: it is not defined if \\( n/L \\) is not an integer The difference with the continuous case is fundamental. Compressing a continuous signal is reversible: you expand it back and it is the same. Decimating a sequence throws samples in the trash, and unless the signal was oversampled, what was lost does not come back. That is why in digital processing decimation is always preceded by an antialiasing filter, and interpolation followed by a reconstruction filter. Changing the sampling rate Going from 48 kHz to 44.1 kHz —from professional audio to compact disc— means multiplying by 147/160. It is done by interpolating by 147 and decimating by 160, with a filter in between. All the machinery of sampling-rate conversion is this transformation, applied carefully."},{"t":"Where each one shows up","d":"Transformations of the independent variable","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/transformaciones-variable#usos","x":"04 Where each one shows up Transformation In a circuit or system \\( x(t - t_0) \\) A delay line, an echo, propagation along a cable \\( x(t) + \\alpha\\,x(t - t_0) \\) A simple echo, and also multipath interference \\( x(-t) \\) Convolution and correlation, where a signal is reflected before it slides \\( x(at) \\) Changing the playback speed, or Doppler shift \\( x[Mn] \\) Lowering the sampling rate of a digital stream The third row is the one that will be used most from here on. Convolution is defined with \\( h(t - \\tau) \\), which is a reflection followed by a shift, and understanding those two operations well is what keeps the convolution integral from being an opaque formula."},{"t":"In the lab","d":"Transformations of the independent variable","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/transformaciones-variable#laboratorio","x":"05 In the lab Exercise 1 · The key points, by hand Given a signal with corners at \\( t = 0 \\), \\( 1 \\) and \\( 1.5 \\), sketch \\( x(2t + 3) \\), \\( x(-t + 1) \\) and \\( x(-2t - 1) \\) by mapping each corner with \\( t = (t_1 - b)/a \\). Check all three with the lab. Exercise 2 · The echo Build with two operational amplifiers and a digital delay line a system \\( y(t) = x(t) + 0.5\\,x(t - 20\\text{ ms}) \\). Observe the effect on a pulse train and on a sinusoid, and explain why periodic nulls appear in frequency. Exercise 3 · Decimating without filtering Sample a 3 kHz sinusoid at 40 kHz and then keep one sample out of every eight. Compare with what you get by filtering at 2.5 kHz first. Explain the difference."},{"t":"Common mistakes","d":"Transformations of the independent variable","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/transformaciones-variable#errores","x":"06 Common mistakes Shifting by \\( b \\) instead of \\( b/a \\). The most frequent mistake of all. The key points avoid it. Reversing the direction of the shift. \\( x(t - t_0) \\) with positive \\( t_0 \\) moves to the right. Believing that \\( x(2t) \\) stretches the signal. It compresses it: it reaches each value sooner. Doing the operations in any order. Scaling and then shifting is not the same as the reverse, unless the shift is corrected. Decimating without filtering first. What is lost does not come back, and on top of that aliasing appears."},{"t":"Self-assessment","d":"Transformations of the independent variable","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/transformaciones-variable#autoevaluacion","x":"07 Self-assessment Which way does \\( x(t - 3) \\) move? To the right: it is a delay of 3 units. If \\( x(t) \\) has a corner at \\( t = 2 \\), where is it in \\( x(2t + 1) \\)? Where \\( 2t + 1 = 2 \\), that is, at \\( t = 0.5 \\). What is the effective shift in \\( x(3t + 6) \\)? \\( b/a = 2 \\) to the left, not 6. Then it is compressed by 3. What does \\( x(-2t) \\) do to the signal? It reflects it and compresses it to half. The sign does the reflection; the magnitude, the scaling. Why is decimation not reversible? Because samples are discarded. Unless the signal was oversampled, the information that lay between the ones that remain is lost. What transformations are inside a convolution? A reflection and a shift: \\( h(t - \\tau) \\) is \\( h \\) mirrored and moved to \\( t \\)."},{"t":"Further reading","d":"Transformations of the independent variable","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/transformaciones-variable#ampliar","x":"08 Further reading Alan V. Oppenheim and Alan S. Willsky. Signals and Systems. 2nd ed. (Spanish edition, “Señales y sistemas”), Prentice Hall, 1998. Section 1.2, with the graphical examples that are the reference for this topic. John G. Proakis and Dimitris G. Manolakis. Digital Signal Processing. 4th ed. (Spanish edition, “Tratamiento digital de señales”), Pearson, 2007. Decimation, interpolation and sampling-rate conversion, with the filters that are needed. Simon Haykin and Barry Van Veen. Signals and Systems. (Spanish edition, “Señales y sistemas”), Limusa Wiley, 2001. A good treatment of the order of operations, with both routes worked out in parallel."},{"t":"Laplace, for sequences","d":"The Z-transform and notions of filtering","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/transformada-z#definicion","x":"01 Laplace, for sequences The Z transform is to discrete signals what Laplace is to continuous ones. The definition replaces the integral with a sum and the exponential with a power: \\[ X(z) = \\sum_{n=-\\infty}^{\\infty} x[n]\\,z^{-n}, \\qquad z \\in \\mathbb{C} \\] Each sample is multiplied by \\( z^{-n} \\). Since \\( z^{-1} \\) is the delay of one sample, the transform is the difference equation written in another way. And the relationship with the \\( s \\)-plane is direct: sampling with period \\( T \\) corresponds to the substitution \\[ z = e^{sT} \\] which takes the imaginary axis of the \\( s \\)-plane to the unit circle of the \\( z \\)-plane, and the left half-plane to the interior of the circle. Everything we knew about stability carries over by replacing “left of the axis” with “inside the circle”. In the \\( s \\)-plane In the \\( z \\)-plane Imaginary axis Unit circle \\( |z| = 1 \\) Left half-plane Interior of the circle Origin \\( s = 0 \\) \\( z = 1 \\), DC \\( \\omega = \\pi/T \\), half the sampling frequency \\( z = -1 \\) Stable: poles on the left Stable: poles inside The fourth row has an important consequence: in the \\( z \\)-plane the whole frequency range fits in one turn. Going around the circle from \\( z = 1 \\) to \\( z = -1 \\) sweeps from DC up to half the sampling frequency, and continuing to turn means starting over. There is aliasing again, turned into geometry."},{"t":"Designing by placing poles and zeros","d":"The Z-transform and notions of filtering","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/transformada-z#lab","x":"02 Designing by placing poles and zeros From the previous relationship comes a direct design technique: a zero on the circle cancels that frequency; a pole near the circle boosts it. With that you build filters by hand, without formulas, just by arranging points. \\[ |H(e^{j\\Omega})| = |K|\\,\\frac{\\prod_i |\\,e^{j\\Omega} - z_i\\,|}{\\prod_k |\\,e^{j\\Omega} - p_k\\,|} \\] The same geometric reading as in the \\( s \\)-plane, but going around the circle instead of along the axis: product of distances to the zeros over product of distances to the poles. Lab · the z-plane and its filter On the left the \\( z \\)-plane with the unit circle, the poles (×) and the zeros (○), and the vectors to the working point. On the right, the magnitude of the response and the impulse response. low-pass · pole near z = 1 high-pass · pole near z = −1 band-pass · pair of poles notch · zeros on the circle comb · several zeros spread around pole outside · unstable Show the vectors Pole radius = 0.90 Filter frequency = 0.20 · fs Observation point = 0.15 · fs"},{"t":"The region of convergence, once again","d":"The Z-transform and notions of filtering","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/transformada-z#roc","x":"03 The region of convergence, once again As in Laplace, \\( X(z) \\) alone is not enough. Except that here the regions are annuli instead of strips: If the region… The sequence is… Is the exterior of the outermost pole Causal: it is zero for \\( n \\lt 0 \\) Is the interior of the innermost pole Anticausal Is an annulus between poles Two-sided Contains the unit circle Has a Fourier transform and the system is stable And the usual conclusion, translated: a causal system is stable if and only if all of its poles are inside the unit circle. The lab shows it with the last case: raising the radius above 1, the impulse response stops dying out and starts to grow. The pair to know by heart \\( a^{n}u[n] \\longleftrightarrow \\dfrac{1}{1 - az^{-1}} \\), with region \\( |z| \\gt |a| \\). From this comes almost everything: the first-order filter, the geometric sum and the condition \\( |a| \\lt 1 \\) for convergence. And its anticausal version, \\( -a^{n}u[-n-1] \\), has exactly the same expression with region \\( |z| \\lt |a| \\)."},{"t":"Basics of digital filtering","d":"The Z-transform and notions of filtering","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/transformada-z#filtrado","x":"04 Basics of digital filtering The geometric reading is enough to design several useful things by eye: What you want What to place where Low-pass Pole near \\( z = 1 \\), zero at \\( z = -1 \\) High-pass Pole near \\( z = -1 \\), zero at \\( z = 1 \\) Reject a frequency \\( f_0 \\) Pair of zeros on the circle at \\( \\pm 2\\pi f_0/f_s \\) Boost a frequency Pair of poles near the circle at that angle Remove DC A zero exactly at \\( z = 1 \\) Comb, for mains harmonics Zeros spread at multiples of the 50 Hz angle The rule of thumb for the bandwidth of a resonator is that a pole of radius \\( r \\) gives a peak whose width at −3 dB is approximately \\( 2(1-r) \\) in radians per sample. With \\( r = 0.99 \\) the peak is extremely narrow; with \\( r = 0.8 \\), wide and smooth. The constraint that cannot be violated If the filter coefficients are real —and they are, because they are programmed with real numbers— the complex poles and zeros have to come in conjugate pairs. You cannot place a lone pole at \\( 0.9e^{j\\pi/4} \\) without its twin at \\( 0.9e^{-j\\pi/4} \\): the resulting filter would need complex arithmetic. That is why all the labs in this topic show the poles in pairs symmetric about the real axis."},{"t":"In the lab","d":"The Z-transform and notions of filtering","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/transformada-z#laboratorio","x":"05 In the lab Activity 1 · A 50 Hz notch by hand With \\( f_s = 1 \\) kHz, calculate the angle that corresponds to 50 Hz and place the pair of zeros on the circle. Add a pair of poles at the same angle with \\( r = 0.95 \\) to narrow the rejection, and write down the coefficients of the resulting biquad. Activity 2 · Measuring the width of the resonator Program \\( y[n] = 2r\\cos\\theta\\,y[n-1] - r^2 y[n-2] + x[n] \\) for \\( r = 0.9 \\), \\( 0.99 \\) and \\( 0.999 \\), measure the −3 dB bandwidth of each and compare it with the rule \\( 2(1-r) \\). Activity 3 · Crossing the circle With the same program, raise \\( r \\) to 1.01 and observe the output. Determine in how many samples a 16-bit accumulator overflows and relate it to the position of the pole."},{"t":"Common mistakes","d":"The Z-transform and notions of filtering","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/transformada-z#errores","x":"06 Common mistakes Placing a complex pole without its conjugate. With real coefficients they must come in pairs. Forgetting the region of convergence. The same \\( X(z) \\) describes a causal or an anticausal sequence depending on the region. Confusing \\( |z| = 1 \\) with the sampling frequency. A full turn is \\( f_s \\); the useful half goes up to \\( z = -1 \\). Placing a pole exactly on the circle. It is an oscillator, not a filter: it is not BIBO stable. Designing in the \\( s \\)-plane and moving to the \\( z \\)-plane carelessly. The bilinear transformation warps the frequency axis, and you have to prewarp."},{"t":"Self-assessment","d":"The Z-transform and notions of filtering","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/transformada-z#autoevaluacion","x":"07 Self-assessment What is the relationship between the \\( s \\) and \\( z \\) planes? \\( z = e^{sT} \\). The imaginary axis goes to the unit circle and the left half-plane to the interior. When is a causal discrete system stable? When all of its poles are inside the unit circle. Where does a zero go to remove DC? At \\( z = 1 \\), which is the point that corresponds to zero frequency. How do you reject a frequency \\( f_0 \\)? With a pair of zeros on the circle at the angle \\( \\pm 2\\pi f_0/f_s \\). How wide is the peak of a pole of radius \\( r \\)? Approximately \\( 2(1-r) \\) radians per sample: the closer to the circle, the narrower. Why do complex poles come in pairs? Because the filter coefficients are real. A lone pole would force the use of complex arithmetic."},{"t":"Further reading","d":"The Z-transform and notions of filtering","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/transformada-z#ampliar","x":"08 Further reading John G. Proakis and Dimitris G. Manolakis. Digital Signal Processing: Principles, Algorithms, and Applications. 4th ed. (Spanish edition, “Tratamiento digital de señales”), Prentice Hall, 2006. The Z transform with its region of convergence, and filter design by pole and zero placement. Alan V. Oppenheim and Ronald W. Schafer. Discrete-Time Signal Processing. 3rd ed. (Spanish edition, “Tratamiento de señales en tiempo discreto”), Prentice Hall, 2010. The standard reference in the field. Chapter 3 is the one on the Z transform. Richard G. Lyons. Understanding Digital Signal Processing. 3rd ed., Prentice Hall, 2010. The geometric reading of the z-plane explained with a clarity that is hard to match."},{"t":"Transmitter architectures","d":"Transmitters","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/transmisores#arquitectura","x":"01 Transmitter architectures A transmitter generates the modulated signal, brings it to the channel frequency and gives it power. The architectures differ in where each step is done: Architecture How it works Advantage and limit Heterodyne Modulates at a fixed intermediate frequency and then mixes up to the channel Easy filtering at the IF; more components Direct conversion The I/Q modulator works directly at the channel frequency Simple and integrable; sensitive to imbalances and to the amplifier \"pulling\" the oscillator Polar Separates amplitude and phase: modulates the oscillator phase and the amplitude through the amplifier's supply Allows efficient nonlinear amplifiers Direct digital A fast converter generates the signal in the final band Maximum flexibility; requires GHz converters The blocks have already been studied: the I/Q modulator, the synthesizer and the power amplifier. The transmitter is the system that combines them while meeting a standard."},{"t":"What the standard requires","d":"Transmitters","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/transmisores#normas","x":"02 What the standard requires The spectrum authority sets what a transmitter may emit. In Argentina, ENACOM (the national communications regulator) certifies equipment; worldwide, the ITU recommendations and the standards of each service define: The power, expressed as effective radiated power or equivalent isotropically radiated power: \\( EIRP = P_t - L_{cable} + G_{antenna} \\), in dBm or dBW. The spectral mask: how much it may emit outside the channel, at different distances from the carrier. The adjacent-channel leakage ratio (ACLR): the power that falls in the neighboring channel relative to that of the channel itself. The spurious and harmonic emissions, far from the channel. The modulation quality, measured as EVM. Adjacent-channel leakage is produced mostly by the nonlinearity of the power amplifier: the third-order intermodulation between the components of the signal itself widens its spectrum."},{"t":"The peak-to-average ratio and efficiency","d":"Transmitters","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/transmisores#pico","x":"03 The peak-to-average ratio and efficiency An FM signal has a constant envelope; a filtered QAM or an OFDM, with many subcarriers, have peaks several dB above their average power. So as not to clip them, the amplifier is sized for the peak and works most of the time far below it, where its efficiency is low. With an ideal class B amplifier whose peak output reaches saturation, in the approximation that the signal is almost always near its average level: \\[ \\eta_B \\approx \\frac{\\pi}{4}\\, 10^{-PAPR/20} \\] \\[ \\eta_A \\approx \\frac{1}{2}\\, 10^{-PAPR/10} \\] PAPR is the ratio between the peak and the average power, in dB. It is an optimistic estimate: the real distribution of the envelope lowers the efficiency somewhat more. Techniques such as the Doherty amplifier or envelope tracking recover a good part of what is lost. Lab · the cost of the peak-to-average ratio A transmitter that has to deliver 10 W average. According to the peak-to-average ratio of the signal, the bars show the power a class B and a class A amplifier draw from the supply, and how much of that power is heat. Peak-to-average ratio ="},{"t":"In the lab","d":"Transmitters","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/transmisores#laboratorio","x":"04 In the lab Activity 1 · Mask Measure with the spectrum analyzer the output of a low-power transmitter and compare it with the mask of its standard. Activity 2 · ACLR Raise the power of an amplifier driven with a QPSK signal and measure how the adjacent-channel leakage grows as it approaches compression. Activity 3 · EIRP Calculate the EIRP of a Wi-Fi link from the equipment power, the cable losses and the antenna gain, and compare it with the regulatory limit of the band."},{"t":"Common mistakes","d":"Transmitters","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/transmisores#errores","x":"05 Common mistakes Sizing the amplifier for the average power. The peaks are clipped and the spectrum widens. Forgetting the cable losses in the EIRP. At high frequencies they can be several dB. Confusing peak power with peak envelope power. The second is the average over one RF cycle at the peak. Measuring the mask with an inadequate RBW. The standards set the measurement bandwidth."},{"t":"Self-assessment","d":"Transmitters","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/transmisores#autoevaluacion","x":"06 Self-assessment What EIRP does a 30 dBm transmitter have with 2 dB of cable loss and a 6 dBi antenna? 34 dBm, about 2.5 W. What efficiency does the formula estimate for class B with 10 dB of PAPR? \\( 78.5 \\% \\cdot 10^{-0.5} \\approx 25 \\% \\). What produces adjacent-channel leakage? The intermodulation of the power amplifier, which widens the spectrum of the signal. What advantage does the polar architecture have? It allows the use of nonlinear amplifiers, which are more efficient, because the amplitude is applied through the supply."},{"t":"Further reading","d":"Transmitters","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/transmisores#ampliar","x":"07 Further reading Qizheng Gu. RF System Design of Transceivers for Wireless Communications. Springer, 2005. Transmitter design with its power, linearity and spur budgets. Steve C. Cripps. RF Power Amplifiers for Wireless Communications. 2nd ed., Artech House, 2006. Efficiency with variable-envelope signals, the Doherty and envelope tracking. Behzad Razavi. RF Microelectronics. 2nd ed., Prentice Hall, 2011. Integrated transmitter architectures and their problems."},{"t":"From material to device","d":"Basic semiconductor theory: carrier transport","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/transporte-portadores#del-material","x":"01 From material to device The page on semiconductor physics established how many carriers there are: the intrinsic concentration \\( n_i \\), on the order of \\( 10^{10} \\) cm⁻³ in silicon at room temperature, the law of mass action \\( n\\,p = n_i^2 \\), and how doping fixes one of the two concentrations. A device, on the other hand, is defined by how those carriers move: what current flows, in which direction, and how long it takes to appear or disappear. There are only two mechanisms that produce current in a semiconductor, drift and diffusion, and two processes that change the number of carriers, generation and recombination. With those four pieces and one equation that ties them together you can explain the diode, the bipolar transistor and the solar cell."},{"t":"Drift and mobility","d":"Basic semiconductor theory: carrier transport","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/transporte-portadores#deriva","x":"02 Drift and mobility An electric field accelerates the carriers, but collisions with the crystal lattice and with impurities slow them down again and again. The net result is an average velocity proportional to the field, and the constant of proportionality is the mobility \\( \\mu \\): \\[ v_n = -\\mu_n E \\qquad v_p = \\mu_p E \\] \\[ J_{\\text{drift}} = q\\,(n\\,\\mu_n + p\\,\\mu_p)\\,E = \\sigma E \\qquad \\rho = \\frac{1}{\\sigma} \\] Electrons move against the field and holes with it, but since their charges have opposite signs the two currents add. In lightly doped silicon at 300 K, \\( \\mu_n \\approx 1400 \\) and \\( \\mu_p \\approx 470 \\) cm²/V·s: electrons are about three times more mobile than holes, and that asymmetry reappears in every device (a PMOS transistor has to be wider than an NMOS to conduct the same current). With 1 V applied across 10 µm, the field is 1000 V/cm and an electron drifts at about 14 km/s. Mobility drops as doping increases, because each ionized impurity is one more scattering center. A widely used empirical fit is the Caughey–Thomas one: \\[ \\mu(N) = \\mu_{\\min} + \\frac{\\mu_{\\max} - \\mu_{\\min}}{1 + \\left( N / N_{\\text{ref}} \\right)^{\\alpha}} \\] For electrons in silicon: \\( \\mu_{\\min} = 68.5 \\), \\( \\mu_{\\max} = 1414 \\) cm²/V·s, \\( N_{\\text{ref}} = 9.2 \\times 10^{16} \\) cm⁻³, \\( \\alpha = 0.711 \\). For holes: 44.9, 470.5, \\( 2.23 \\times 10^{17} \\) and 0.719. Proportionality has a ceiling \\( v = \\mu E \\) holds for moderate fields. Above about \\( 10^4 \\) V/cm the velocity "},{"t":"Diffusion and the Einstein relation","d":"Basic semiconductor theory: carrier transport","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/transporte-portadores#difusion","x":"03 Diffusion and the Einstein relation With no applied field there can still be current. If the carrier concentration is not uniform, their random thermal motion carries more carriers from the crowded region to the sparse one than the other way around: this is diffusion, the same process that spreads a drop of ink in a glass of water. The current is proportional to the concentration gradient: \\[ J_{n,\\text{diff}} = q\\,D_n \\frac{dn}{dx} \\qquad J_{p,\\text{diff}} = -q\\,D_p \\frac{dp}{dx} \\] \\( D \\) is the diffusion coefficient, in cm²/s. The minus sign for holes: they diffuse toward where there are fewer of them, and they carry positive charge. Drift and diffusion are not independent: both are limited by the same collisions. Einstein showed that the ratio between the diffusion coefficient and the mobility is the thermal voltage: \\[ \\frac{D}{\\mu} = \\frac{k\\,T}{q} = V_T \\approx 25.85 \\text{ mV at } 300 \\text{ K} \\] With \\( \\mu_n = 1414 \\) cm²/V·s, \\( D_n \\approx 36.6 \\) cm²/s; with \\( \\mu_p = 470.5 \\), \\( D_p \\approx 12.2 \\) cm²/s. The total current of each carrier is the sum of the two contributions. These two expressions are the starting point of all device theory: \\[ J_n = q\\,n\\,\\mu_n E + q\\,D_n \\frac{dn}{dx} \\] \\[ J_p = q\\,p\\,\\mu_p E - q\\,D_p \\frac{dp}{dx} \\] The thermal voltage \\( V_T \\) appears here for the first time and never goes away: it is in the diode equation, in the transconductance of the bipolar transistor and in the subthreshold slope of the MOSFET."},{"t":"Generation, recombination and lifetime","d":"Basic semiconductor theory: carrier transport","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/transporte-portadores#recombinacion","x":"04 Generation, recombination and lifetime At equilibrium, electron-hole pairs are generated by thermal agitation at the same rate at which they recombine, and \\( n\\,p = n_i^2 \\). If something injects extra carriers (light, a forward-biased junction), the excess recombines and the concentration returns to equilibrium. Under low injection —the excess is much smaller than the majority-carrier concentration— the minority carrier sets the rate, and recombination is proportional to the excess: \\[ R = \\frac{\\Delta n}{\\tau_n} \\qquad L_n = \\sqrt{D_n\\,\\tau_n} \\] \\( \\tau \\) is the minority-carrier lifetime; \\( L \\), the diffusion length: how far they travel on average before recombining. Silicon has an indirect band gap: an electron cannot fall directly into a hole emitting a photon, and it almost always recombines through defects with levels inside the band gap (the Shockley–Read–Hall mechanism). That is why the lifetime depends more on purity than on the material: it ranges from milliseconds in very pure silicon to nanoseconds when it is deliberately contaminated with gold or platinum to make fast diodes. With \\( D_n = 36.6 \\) cm²/s and \\( \\tau_n = 1 \\) µs, the diffusion length is about 60 µm. Device What is wanted Why Solar cell Long \\( \\tau \\), \\( L \\) of hundreds of µm Carriers generated by light have to reach the junction before they recombine Bipolar transistor Base much thinner than \\( L \\) Electrons injected by the emitter have to cross the base without being lost Fast switchin"},{"t":"The continuity equation","d":"Basic semiconductor theory: carrier transport","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/transporte-portadores#continuidad","x":"05 The continuity equation The previous pieces come together in a balance: in a small piece of semiconductor, the number of electrons changes by what comes in minus what goes out, plus what is generated, minus what recombines: \\[ \\frac{\\partial n}{\\partial t} = \\frac{1}{q} \\frac{\\partial J_n}{\\partial x} + G_n - R_n \\] Together with the current expressions and Poisson's equation for the field, this is the system that device simulators solve. The most useful case is the steady state, with no field, with minority carriers injected at one end. The equation becomes \\( D\\,\\dfrac{d^2 \\Delta p}{dx^2} = \\dfrac{\\Delta p}{\\tau} \\), and its solution is an excess that decays exponentially with the diffusion length: \\[ \\Delta p(x) = \\Delta p(0)\\; e^{-x/L_p} \\] At one diffusion length from the edge, 37 % of the excess remains; at three, 5 %. That profile is exactly what happens on each side of a forward-biased junction, and from its slope at the edge comes the diode current, as seen in the next unit."},{"t":"Where it appears in electronics","d":"Basic semiconductor theory: carrier transport","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/transporte-portadores#electronica","x":"06 Where it appears in electronics Case The mechanism Integrated resistors A diffused strip of known sheet resistance: the resistivity divided by the thickness Measuring the doping of a wafer The four-point probe measures the resistivity and the Irvin curve gives the concentration Hall-effect sensor The drift of carriers deflected by a magnetic field; the sensitivity grows with mobility Photoconductor Light generates pairs and the conductivity rises for as long as their lifetime lasts Fast semiconductors (GaAs, InP) Electron mobilities several times that of silicon, for radio frequency and microwaves"},{"t":"In the lab","d":"Basic semiconductor theory: carrier transport","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/transporte-portadores#laboratorio","x":"07 In the lab Activity 1 · Four-point resistivity Use four collinear, equally spaced probes to measure a silicon wafer and calculate \\( \\rho = 2\\pi s\\,V/I \\) (valid for thicknesses large compared with the spacing \\( s \\); otherwise, apply the correction factor). Deduce the doping from the curve in this page's lab. Activity 2 · A semiconductor that conducts with light Bias a photoresistor (LDR) with a source and a series resistor, illuminate it with a pulsed LED and use the oscilloscope to see how long the resistance takes to return to its dark value when the light is turned off. Activity 3 · The thermistor as a semiconductor Measure the resistance of an NTC thermistor between 0 °C and 80 °C and plot the logarithm of \\( R \\) against \\( 1/T \\). Estimate the activation energy from the slope and compare it with the band gap of the material."},{"t":"Common mistakes","d":"Basic semiconductor theory: carrier transport","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/transporte-portadores#errores","x":"08 Common mistakes Thinking that more doping always gives proportionally more conduction. The conductivity grows less than the doping, because the mobility drops. Using the same mobility for electrons and holes. In silicon they differ by a factor of three. Forgetting diffusion where there is no field. In the base of a bipolar transistor almost all of the current is diffusion current. Applying \\( v = \\mu E \\) at high fields. The velocity saturates near \\( 10^7 \\) cm/s. Confusing lifetime with transit time. The first is how long a minority carrier lives; the second, how long it takes to cross a region."},{"t":"Self-assessment","d":"Basic semiconductor theory: carrier transport","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/transporte-portadores#autoevaluacion","x":"09 Self-assessment What are the two current mechanisms in a semiconductor and what drives them? Drift, driven by the electric field, and diffusion, driven by the concentration gradient. Why does mobility decrease with doping? Because each ionized impurity is an additional scattering center for the carriers. If \\( \\mu_p = 470 \\) cm²/V·s, what is \\( D_p \\) at 300 K? \\( D_p = \\mu_p V_T = 470 \\times 0.02585 \\approx 12.2 \\) cm²/s. With \\( D = 12 \\) cm²/s and \\( \\tau = 10 \\) µs, what is the diffusion length? \\( L = \\sqrt{12 \\times 10^{-5}} \\approx 1.1 \\times 10^{-2} \\) cm, about 110 µm. Why is the silicon of a fast diode contaminated with gold? Gold introduces levels in the middle of the band gap that speed up recombination and shorten the lifetime, so the stored charge disappears sooner."},{"t":"Further reading","d":"Basic semiconductor theory: carrier transport","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/transporte-portadores#ampliar","x":"10 Further reading Robert F. Pierret. Semiconductor Device Fundamentals. Addison-Wesley, 1996. Drift, diffusion and the continuity equation developed patiently, with worked problems. Donald A. Neamen. Semiconductor Physics and Devices. 4th ed., McGraw-Hill, 2012. The most widely used course text; its chapters on transport and excess carriers are the basis of this unit. Simon M. Sze and Kwok K. Ng. Physics of Semiconductor Devices. 3rd ed., Wiley, 2007. The reference book, with the measured mobility and resistivity curves."},{"t":"Three ways of sharing electrons","d":"Chemical bonding","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/uniones-quimicas#tres","x":"01 Three ways of sharing electrons Atoms bond because doing so lowers their energy, and they do it in three ways depending on how hard they contest the electrons. What decides it is electronegativity: the tendency of each atom to pull the bond's electrons toward itself. Bond Difference \\( \\Delta\\chi \\) What happens Example Ionic \\( \\gt 1.7 \\) One tears them away from the other: ions are left NaCl, metal oxides Polar covalent \\( 0.4 \\) to \\( 1.7 \\) They share them, but unevenly H₂O, SiO₂ Nonpolar covalent \\( \\lt 0.4 \\) They share them equally Si—Si, diamond Metallic Between metals The electrons come loose and belong to all Cu, Al, Au The third row is the bond of silicon with itself: four electrons shared with four neighbors, all committed, none free. And the fourth explains why metals conduct: their valence electrons do not belong to any particular atom, but form a cloud that moves under any applied field. Weak bonds count too Between molecules act much weaker forces —dipole–dipole, hydrogen bonding, London— that do not form compounds but do determine the melting point, the viscosity and the adhesion. On them depend flux wetting the surface, an epoxy resin sticking, and isopropyl alcohol carrying away dirt and then evaporating by itself."},{"t":"From bonds to bands","d":"Chemical bonding","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/uniones-quimicas#bandas","x":"02 From bonds to bands In an isolated atom the electrons have discrete levels. When two atoms are brought together, each level splits in two; with \\( N \\) atoms, into \\( N \\) levels so close together that they form a continuous band. That is what happens in a crystal, where \\( N \\) is on the order of \\( 10^{23} \\). Between two bands there may be a gap of forbidden energies, the band gap or gap. And all electrical conduction is explained by two questions: whether the valence band is full, and how wide the gap is that separates it from the next one. Material Bands It conducts because… Metal The last band is half full There are empty states right next to it: a minimal field moves the electrons Insulator Full valence band, gap of more than 4 eV It does not conduct: no electron gathers enough energy to jump Semiconductor Full valence band, gap of 0.5 to 3 eV At room temperature a few jump, and doping controls how many How many electrons jump through thermal agitation comes from an exponential, and that is the formula that governs the behavior of every solid-state device: \\[ n_i = \\sqrt{N_c N_v}\\;e^{-E_g/2kT} \\] With \\( kT \\approx 0.0259 \\) eV at 300 K. That \\( E_g \\) is in the exponent is the reason why a difference of half an electronvolt changes the conductivity by several orders of magnitude. Lab · bands, temperature and carriers The band gap drawn to scale and the carriers that temperature manages to promote. Change the material and the temperature, and watch the orders of mag"},{"t":"What doping does to the bands","d":"Chemical bonding","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/uniones-quimicas#dopaje","x":"03 What doping does to the bands A phosphorus atom in the lattice contributes an electron that finds no bond to fit into. That electron sits in a level of its own, only 0.045 eV below the conduction band, and with such a small distance thermal agitation promotes it effortlessly: at room temperature, practically all the dopants are ionized. Level Distance to its band Compare with Phosphorus in silicon, donor 0.045 eV below the conduction band \\( kT = 0.026 \\) eV Boron in silicon, acceptor 0.045 eV above the valence band It ionizes just as easily Gold in silicon, trap 0.54 eV, in the middle It does not contribute carriers: it captures them The third row shows the other side. An impurity with a level in the middle of the gap does not dope: it acts as a recombination center, trapping electron–hole pairs. That is a misfortune in an ordinary transistor, and it is used on purpose in fast diodes, where it matters that the carriers disappear as soon as possible so that switching can be faster."},{"t":"In the lab","d":"Chemical bonding","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/uniones-quimicas#laboratorio","x":"04 In the lab Activity 1 · Predict the bond Look up the electronegativities of Si, O, Na, Cl, Cu and Ga, calculate \\( \\Delta\\chi \\) for SiO₂, NaCl, GaAs and a Cu–Zn alloy, and classify each bond. Compare with the known properties of each material. Activity 2 · Germanium gives up under heat Measure the reverse current of a germanium diode and a silicon diode while heating them with a hot-air gun from 25 to 90 °C. Plot on a logarithmic scale and compare the slopes with what \\( e^{-E_g/2kT} \\) predicts. Activity 3 · The resistance of the NTC Measure the resistance of an NTC thermistor between 0 and 80 °C, linearize with \\( \\ln R \\) against \\( 1/T \\) and obtain its constant \\( \\beta \\). Relate that constant to the band gap of the thermistor material."},{"t":"Common mistakes","d":"Chemical bonding","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/uniones-quimicas#errores","x":"05 Common mistakes Believing that a bond is either ionic or covalent. It is a continuum; almost all bonds have something of both. Thinking that an insulator never conducts. It conducts with sufficient fields: that is dielectric breakdown. Confusing the band gap with a potential barrier. One belongs to the material; the other, to the junction. Forgetting that \\( n_i \\) depends exponentially on temperature. Doubling the degrees does not double the carriers: it multiplies them. Assuming that every impurity dopes. Deep-level ones capture carriers instead of contributing them."},{"t":"Self-assessment","d":"Chemical bonding","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/uniones-quimicas#autoevaluacion","x":"06 Self-assessment What type of bond does SiO₂ have? Polar covalent: \\( \\Delta\\chi \\approx 1.5 \\), close to the boundary with the ionic. Why does a metal conduct? Because its last band is half full: there are empty states right next to the occupied ones and any field moves the electrons. What is the value of \\( kT \\) at room temperature? 0.026 eV, about 26 mV. It is the number that appears in the diode equation. Why does germanium fail at 70 °C and silicon does not? Because its band gap is almost half as wide, and \\( n_i \\) grows exponentially: at that temperature it already conducts by itself and the doping stops being in charge. What does gold do in silicon? It creates a deep level that captures electron–hole pairs. It is used deliberately to make fast diodes. Why are SiC and GaN suitable for power applications? Their wide band gap lets them work at temperatures and voltages where silicon already conducts on its own."},{"t":"Further reading","d":"Chemical bonding","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/uniones-quimicas#ampliar","x":"07 Further reading Raymond Chang and Kenneth A. Goldsby. Chemistry. 12th ed. (Spanish edition, “Química”), McGraw-Hill, 2017. Chapters 9 and 10: ionic, covalent and metallic bonding, with the electronegativity scale. William D. Callister and David G. Rethwisch. Materials Science and Engineering: An Introduction. 9th ed. (Spanish edition, “Introducción a la ciencia e ingeniería de los materiales”), Reverté, 2019. The step from bonds to bands, with the comparison between metals, ceramics and polymers. Ben G. Streetman and Sanjay Banerjee. Solid State Electronic Devices. 7th ed., Pearson, 2015. Bands, intrinsic concentration and impurity levels, with the numerical values for silicon."},{"t":"What the state of a circuit is","d":"Solving circuits with state variables","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/variables-estado#estado","x":"01 What the state of a circuit is To predict what a circuit will do from some instant onward you do not need to know its entire history: it is enough to know how much energy each element that can store energy holds. That minimal information is the state, and in an electric circuit it has a natural choice: the capacitor voltages and the inductor currents, which are precisely the quantities that cannot jump suddenly. The number of state variables is the order of the circuit: an RC is first order, an RLC second order. It coincides with the number of poles that were counted in the \\( s \\)-plane."},{"t":"The state equations","d":"Solving circuits with state variables","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/variables-estado#ecuaciones","x":"02 The state equations Writing Kirchhoff’s laws so that each equation solves for the derivative of one state variable, the whole circuit becomes a system of first-order differential equations, in matrix form: \\[ \\dot{\\mathbf{x}} = \\mathbf{A}\\,\\mathbf{x} + \\mathbf{B}\\,u \\] \\[ y = \\mathbf{C}\\,\\mathbf{x} + D\\,u \\] \\( \\mathbf{x} \\) is the state vector, \\( u \\) the input, \\( y \\) the output. All the dynamics of the circuit lie in the matrix \\( \\mathbf{A} \\): its eigenvalues are exactly the poles of the circuit. For the series RLC of the transient response unit, with \\( x_1 = v_C \\) and \\( x_2 = i_L \\): \\[ \\begin{bmatrix} \\dot v_C \\\\ \\dot i_L \\end{bmatrix} = \\begin{bmatrix} 0 & 1/C \\\\ -1/L & -R/L \\end{bmatrix} \\begin{bmatrix} v_C \\\\ i_L \\end{bmatrix} + \\begin{bmatrix} 0 \\\\ 1/L \\end{bmatrix} V \\] The first row is the capacitor equation, \\( i = C\\,dv/dt \\); the second, the mesh equation solved for \\( L\\,di/dt \\). The characteristic polynomial of \\( \\mathbf{A} \\) is \\( \\lambda^2 + (R/L)\\lambda + 1/LC \\), the same denominator as \\( V_C(s) \\)."},{"t":"The phase plane","d":"Solving circuits with state variables","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/variables-estado#plano","x":"03 The phase plane With two state variables, the state at each instant is a point of the \\( (v_C, i_L) \\) plane, and the evolution of the circuit is a curve: the trajectory. An overdamped circuit reaches equilibrium along a curve with no turns; an underdamped one, along a spiral. Looking at the trajectory shows at a glance how the energy is shared between the capacitor and the inductor at each moment."},{"t":"Solving step by step: this is how a simulator simulates","d":"Solving circuits with state variables","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/variables-estado#numerica","x":"04 Solving step by step: this is how a simulator simulates The state form lends itself to solving the circuit numerically, advancing in small time steps \\( h \\). The simplest method, Euler’s, uses the current derivative to take the step: \\[ \\mathbf{x}_{k+1} = \\mathbf{x}_k + h\\,(\\mathbf{A}\\mathbf{x}_k + \\mathbf{B}u_k) \\] Euler is stable for a pole \\( \\lambda \\) only if \\( |1 + h\\lambda| Lab · the same RLC, integrated step by step Series RLC of 1 mH and 100 nF connected to 5 V. On the left, the phase plane; on the right, \\( v_C(t) \\). In gray the exact solution, computed with residues; in color, the numerical integration with the chosen method and step. R = 30 Ω · ζ = 0.15 R = 60 Ω · ζ = 0.3 R = 120 Ω · ζ = 0.6 R = 300 Ω · ζ = 1.5 Explicit Euler 4th-order Runge-Kutta step h = 0.05/ωn (0.5 µs) step h = 0.2/ωn (2 µs) step h = 0.5/ωn (5 µs) step h = 1/ωn (10 µs) step h = 2/ωn (20 µs) Real simulators do not use explicit Euler but implicit methods —trapezoidal, Gear’s— that are stable with any step, and they also adjust \\( h \\) by themselves: they shrink it where the signal changes quickly and enlarge it where nothing is happening. When a SPICE simulation aborts with “timestep too small,” what it is saying is that it could not find a step small enough to follow the circuit."},{"t":"Where it shows up in electronics","d":"Solving circuits with state variables","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/variables-estado#electronica","x":"05 Where it shows up in electronics Case What the state form contributes Transient analysis in SPICE Numerically integrates the state variables of the whole circuit Control of a switching converter The averaged state-space model is the basis of loop design Nonlinear or time-varying circuits Where Laplace does not apply, the state form keeps working Firmware of a digital filter or an observer Directly implements \\( \\mathbf{x}_{k+1} = \\mathbf{A}_d\\mathbf{x}_k + \\mathbf{B}_d u_k \\) on a microcontroller"},{"t":"In the lab","d":"Solving circuits with state variables","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/variables-estado#laboratorio","x":"06 In the lab Activity 1 · Eigenvalues and poles Calculate the eigenvalues of \\( \\mathbf{A} \\) for R = 60 Ω and compare them with the poles given by the transient response lab for the same resistance. Activity 2 · The limit of Euler Using this page’s lab, find for each value of R the step beyond which Euler becomes unstable, and verify the criterion \\( h Activity 3 · Writing the integrator Program Euler’s method for the RLC in Python or C and plot \\( v_C(t) \\). Repeat with the trapezoidal method and compare the stability with the same step."},{"t":"Common mistakes","d":"Solving circuits with state variables","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/variables-estado#errores","x":"07 Common mistakes Choosing as state quantities that can jump. The current of a capacitor or the voltage of an inductor are not good state variables. Counting the order wrong. Two capacitors in parallel are a single state; the order is the number of independent storage elements. Trusting a simulation without looking at the step. A result that oscillates and grows can be a numerical artifact and not the circuit. Believing that shrinking the step is always enough. It improves accuracy, but with explicit methods and widely separated poles the cost becomes prohibitive; that is why implicit methods exist."},{"t":"Self-assessment","d":"Solving circuits with state variables","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/variables-estado#autoevaluacion","x":"08 Self-assessment Which state variables are chosen in an electric circuit? The capacitor voltages and the inductor currents. What is the relationship between the matrix A and the poles? The eigenvalues of A are the poles of the circuit. What shape does the trajectory of an underdamped circuit have in the phase plane? A spiral that converges to the equilibrium point. When is Euler’s method stable for the RLC? When \\( h Why do simulators use implicit methods? Because they are stable with any step, which allows large steps where the signal changes little."},{"t":"Further reading","d":"Solving circuits with state variables","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/variables-estado#ampliar","x":"09 Further reading Katsuhiko Ogata. Modern Control Engineering. 5th ed., Prentice Hall, 2010 (Spanish edition, “Ingeniería de control moderna”, Pearson). The state-space representation, its solution and the relationship with the transfer function. Leon O. Chua and Pen-Min Lin. Computer-Aided Analysis of Electronic Circuits. Prentice Hall, 1975. The classic book on how simulators integrate the state equations and why they choose implicit methods. Charles K. Alexander and Matthew N. O. Sadiku. Fundamentals of Electric Circuits. 7th ed., McGraw-Hill, 2021. The introduction to state variables in circuits, with worked examples."},{"t":"When one number is not enough","d":"Vectors in R² and R³","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/vectores-r2-r3#escalar-vector","x":"01 When one number is not enough The temperature of a room, the resistance of a resistor or the energy of a battery can be described with a number and a unit: they are scalars. The force on an electron, the electric field at a point or the velocity of a moving object also need a direction and a sense: they are vectors. A vector is drawn as an arrow, but what matters is its set of three data: magnitude, direction and sense. Two arrows drawn in different places represent the same vector if they have the same magnitude, the same direction and the same sense; that is why they are called free vectors. Two products, not one Between vectors there are two different products, and they must not be confused: the scalar (or dot) product, which gives a number, and the vector (or cross) product, which gives another vector. Each one answers a different physical question: how much one vector goes along with the other, and how perpendicular they are."},{"t":"Components, magnitude and unit vectors","d":"Vectors in R² and R³","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/vectores-r2-r3#componentes","x":"02 Components, magnitude and unit vectors In a set of perpendicular axes, a vector is written with its projections onto each axis: \\[ \\vec{v} = (v_x, v_y, v_z) = v_x \\hat{i} + v_y \\hat{j} + v_z \\hat{k} \\quad \\left| \\vec{v} \\right| = \\sqrt{v_x^2 + v_y^2 + v_z^2} \\] The magnitude is the Pythagorean theorem in three dimensions. The unit vectors î, ĵ and k̂ have magnitude 1 and point along the axes. The unit vector of a vector is that same vector divided by its magnitude: it keeps the direction and the sense, and has length 1. It is used to separate “how much” from “which way”, which is one of the most useful operations in vector calculus. Example For v = (3, −4, 0): |v| = √(9 + 16) = 5, and its unit vector is (0.6, −0.8, 0). Check: 0.6² + 0.8² = 0.36 + 0.64 = 1. ✔"},{"t":"Addition and scalar multiplication","d":"Vectors in R² and R³","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/vectores-r2-r3#operaciones","x":"03 Addition and scalar multiplication They are added component by component, which is the same as the parallelogram rule: draw the two vectors from a common origin and take the diagonal. Multiplying by a scalar k stretches the vector by a factor of k, and if k is negative it reverses the sense. A linear combination mixes the two operations: a·u + b·v. It is the idea that is later generalized in vector spaces, and it is also what a circuit does when it superposes the response to two sources."},{"t":"Dot product: how much one goes along with the other","d":"Vectors in R² and R³","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/vectores-r2-r3#escalar","x":"04 Dot product: how much one goes along with the other \\[ \\vec{u} \\cdot \\vec{v} = u_x v_x + u_y v_y + u_z v_z = \\left| \\vec{u} \\right| \\left| \\vec{v} \\right| \\cos \\theta \\] Two formulas for the same thing: one is computed from the components and the other explains what it means. Equating them gives the angle between the vectors. It is zero if and only if the vectors are perpendicular (with neither one zero). It is the quickest way to check orthogonality. It is positive if the angle is acute and negative if it is obtuse. It is commutative and distributive, and u · u = |u|². The projection of u onto v —the “shadow” of u in the direction of v— comes straight out of it: \\[ {\\operatorname{proj}}_v \\vec{u} = \\frac{\\vec{u} \\cdot \\vec{v}}{{\\left| \\vec{v} \\right|}^2} \\vec{v} \\] In physics, the work done by a force is W = F · d: only the component of the force that goes along with the displacement does work. In alternating current, the active power is P = |V||I| cos φ, which is exactly the dot product between the voltage and current phasors."},{"t":"Cross product: how perpendicular they are","d":"Vectors in R² and R³","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/vectores-r2-r3#vectorial","x":"05 Cross product: how perpendicular they are It exists only in three-dimensional space. It gives a vector perpendicular to the plane of the two, with the sense given by the right-hand rule, and it is computed as a symbolic determinant: \\[ \\vec{u} \\times \\vec{v} = \\left| \\begin{array}{ccc} \\hat{i} & \\hat{j} & \\hat{k} \\\\ u_x & u_y & u_z \\\\ v_x & v_y & v_z \\end{array} \\right| \\quad \\left| \\vec{u} \\times \\vec{v} \\right| = \\left| \\vec{u} \\right| \\left| \\vec{v} \\right| \\operatorname{sin} \\theta \\] Its magnitude is the area of the parallelogram they form. It is zero if they are parallel: then there is no parallelogram. It is not commutative u × v = −(v × u): reversing the order reverses the sense of the result. Nor is it associative. It is the operation that explains why the force on a moving charge in a magnetic field, F = q·v × B, is perpendicular to both the velocity and the field, and why a motor turns one way and not the other. The scalar triple product u · (v × w) is a number: the determinant of the matrix of the three vectors’ components, and in absolute value, the volume of the parallelepiped. If it is zero, the three vectors are coplanar, which is the same as saying they are linearly dependent."},{"t":"Seeing it in the plane","d":"Vectors in R² and R³","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/vectores-r2-r3#laboratorio-vec","x":"06 Seeing it in the plane Drag the tips of the two vectors. Everything above is recalculated live: the sum by the parallelogram, the dot product, the angle, the projection and the area they define. Lab · two vectors in the plane Make them perpendicular Make them parallel Show the sum The vectors Products"},{"t":"Vectors in electronics","d":"Vectors in R² and R³","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/vectores-r2-r3#electronica","x":"07 Vectors in electronics Phasors. A sinusoidal voltage is represented by a rotating vector; adding two signals of the same frequency means adding vectors. This is the subject of the vector study of alternating current. Power. The power triangle —active, reactive and apparent— is a vector sum: S² = P² + Q². The active power comes from the dot product between voltage and current; the reactive power, from the perpendicular part. Fields. The electric and magnetic fields are vector fields, and Maxwell’s equations are written with dot and cross products. The Poynting vector, which indicates where the energy of a wave flows, is S = E × H. Mechanics in the lab. Forces on a bracket, moments in a motor, accelerations: all of Physics I is applied vector algebra."},{"t":"In the lab","d":"Vectors in R² and R³","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/vectores-r2-r3#laboratorio","x":"08 In the lab Exercise 1 · Angle between two vectors Given u = (2, 3, −1) and v = (−1, 4, 2), compute by hand the dot product, the magnitudes, the angle and the projection of u onto v. Verify the angle with the cross product: |u × v| = |u||v| sin θ has to give the same θ. Exercise 2 · Area and volume Compute the area of the triangle with vertices A(1,0,2), B(3,1,0) and C(0,2,1) as half the magnitude of the cross product of two of its sides. Then add a fourth point and use the scalar triple product to decide whether the four are coplanar. Exercise 3 · The right-hand rule with the multimeter With a coil and a magnet, check the direction of the induced voltage when moving the magnet and compare it with what the cross product in Faraday’s law predicts. An oscilloscope or the instrument simulator helps you see the sign of the pulse."},{"t":"Common mistakes","d":"Vectors in R² and R³","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/vectores-r2-r3#errores","x":"09 Common mistakes Adding magnitudes. |u + v| is not |u| + |v| unless they are parallel and have the same sense. Confusing the two products: the dot product gives a number, the cross product a vector. Reversing the order in the cross product and ending up with the sense flipped. Using the cross product in the plane without stating that you are working in R³ with z = 0. Forgetting to normalize when projecting: the projection has |v|² in the denominator, not |v|. Mixing degrees and radians when computing the angle with the calculator."},{"t":"Self-assessment","d":"Vectors in R² and R³","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/vectores-r2-r3#autoevaluacion","x":"10 Self-assessment Compute u · v for u = (2, 3, −1) and v = (−1, 4, 2). −2 + 12 − 2 = 8. Since it is positive, the angle between them is acute. What is the unit vector of (0, 3, 4)? Its magnitude is 5, so the unit vector is (0, 0.6, 0.8). When is the cross product zero? When the vectors are parallel or antiparallel, or when either one is the zero vector. Then sin θ = 0 and the parallelogram degenerates. Compute î × ĵ and ĵ × î. î × ĵ = k̂ and ĵ × î = −k̂: the cross product is anticommutative. If u · v = 0 and neither is zero, what angle do they form? 90°: they are perpendicular. What does it mean for the scalar triple product of three vectors to be zero? That they are coplanar: the parallelepiped has zero volume and the three are linearly dependent."},{"t":"Further reading","d":"Vectors in R² and R³","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/vectores-r2-r3#ampliar","x":"11 Further reading Charles H. Lehmann. Analytic Geometry. (Spanish edition, “Geometría analítica”), Limusa. The classic for the geometric part: vectors, lines, planes and conics in great detail. Stanley I. Grossman. Álgebra lineal. 6th ed., McGraw-Hill, 2008 (in Spanish). Treats the vectors of R² and R³ as a gateway to vector spaces. Murray R. Spiegel. Schaum’s Outline of Vector Analysis. 2nd ed. (Spanish edition, “Análisis vectorial”, Schaum series), McGraw-Hill, 2009. For practice: hundreds of worked exercises, and it goes on to gradient, divergence and curl. Raymond A. Serway and John W. Jewett. Physics for Scientists and Engineers. 9th ed. (Spanish edition, “Física para ciencias e ingeniería”), Cengage Learning, 2014. Where they are really used: forces, fields, torques and the cross product in magnetism."}]);
